  "title": "TCS Capex & Depreciation: Walk the Three Statements",
  "scenario": "TCS (Tata Consultancy Services) is planning a ₹50 crore investment in new data centre infrastructure with a 10-year life. Your manager asks you to model how this capex flows through all three statements over Year 1, and separately, how a ₹10 crore depreciation change ripples through P&L, cash flow, and balance sheet. You'll build the walk to ace the 'depreciation up ₹10 — explain it' interview question.",
  "instructions": "Build formulas to trace the ₹10 depreciation walk (P&L, CFO, balance sheet impacts) and the ₹50 capex one-year effects. Compute PAT, CFO add-back, and prove the balance sheet balances after each scenario.",
  "learning_objective": "Trace how a ₹10 depreciation change affects EBIT, PAT, CFO and PP&E across three statements, and explain why capex leaves the P&L unaffected on day one but visits later as depreciation.",
  "hints": [
    "Start with P&L: depreciation ₹10 reduces EBIT by ₹10. Then apply the tax rate to find the tax shield.",
    "In the cash flow, PAT falls but depreciation is added back as non-cash. The net CFO change equals the tax saved.",
    "On the balance sheet, PP&E shrinks by depreciation, cash rises by the tax shield, and retained earnings fall by (depreciation × (1 − tax rate))."
  ],
